Subject: Issues with model fit and density predictions
Message-ID: <42CqhFsiqwI.hp6m2wxEAgAJ@distance-sampling.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/distance-sampling/c/42CqhFsiqwI/m/hp6m2wxEAgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi all,

I’m a grad student working on a single species study
collecting point count transect data with distance sampling methods.  I’ve run into a few issues I was hoping you
all would be willing to provide feedback on.

We have two years of point count survey data collected.  In the first year we collected distances on
only visual observations.  In year two,
however, we collected distances for both visual and auditory detections of our
study species.  The goal is to use
several years of survey data to create a two-stage model of habitat use
following the procedure laid out in Buckland et al 2009.  In order to perform distance analysis on the
data from both years, I’ve decided to only use our visual detections. I also
opted to pool the data from both years because the sample size in each year was
not very high (year 1: ~80 detections, year 2: ~90 detections).  In total I have 176 observations from both
years combined.

                The
upper limit of our distance detections is just beyond 300 meters.  After truncating the farthest 10-15% of the
data, I still seem to have a long tail of distant detections.  Estimating models using this truncation
distance results in poor fit in the uniform and half normal models (visual
inspection and GOF tests).  The hazard
rate fits better, presumably because it is more flexible, but produces
estimates of N and D that are unrealistically high (density = 479 birds per square km).  All models have estimates of detectability
below 0.08 which also seems far lower than I’d expect in reality.

                After
truncating the data at several distances I found that models seem to fit well
with a truncation distance of 70 meters.
This truncation distance only leaves 69% of the detections for analysis
though.  Additionally, all models with a
truncation distance of 70 meters produce unrealistically high estimates of N.  One trend I noticed was that as I decreased truncation distance, density estimates tended to increase across all models.

                To
summarize the problem, models with far truncation distances produce more
reasonable estimates of density and abundance (except for Hazard rate) but they
don’t seem to fit the data (from visual inspection and GOF tests). These models also produce very low estimates of detectability. Models with more extreme truncation distances
fit the data well but produce unrealistically high estimates of density and abundance.

                I guess
my questions are:

1)
Am I making an error by truncating my data set
down to just 70 meters?  Is this a case
of good fit-bad predictions?

2)
Is there any recommended action when a model
provides good fit but produces density estimates that seem to be inflated?

3)
In one study of Island Scrub Jays, Sillett et al
2012, the authors binned their data into three very wide bins (0-100m, 100-200m,
200-300m) to account for responsive movement of birds during their
surveys.  Would this be a reasonable
approach to use in this situation?

Here is the paper cited: Sillett,
T. S., Chandler, R. B., Royle, J. A., Kéry, M., & Morrison, S. A. (2012).
Hierarchical distance‐sampling
models to estimate population size and habitat‐specific abundance of an island endemic. Ecological Applications, 22(7), 1997-2006.

                I'm concerned that there may be issues with the distribution of our detections that
could result from a violation of assumptions or lack of visibility in dense
forest habitat.  Maybe birds are moving
responsively or there are instances of recounting.  Since we only have single observer data I’m
not sure how I can investigate this.

I’m happy to provide more detail on the models.  Thanks for any insights you can give!

All the best,

Tommy McLaren

From u1fb54f491646a589 at groups.google.com  Sun Mar 21 10:03:21 2021
From: u1fb54f491646a589 at groups.google.com (Stephen Buckland)
Date: Sun, 21 Mar 2021 10:03:21 +0000
Subject: RE: {Suspected Spam} [distance-sampling] Issues with model fit and density predictions
Message-ID: <42CqhFsiqwI.iLIxPLFXAgAJ@distance-sampling.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <42CqhFsiqwI.hp6m2wxEAgAJ@distance-sampling.groups.google.com>
References: <42CqhFsiqwI.hp6m2wxEAgAJ@distance-sampling.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/distance-sampling/c/42CqhFsiqwI/m/iLIxPLFXAgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Tommy, when detection probabilities are highly heterogeneous, you can get long-tailed distributions like this, then more severe truncation is sensible.  You hint at the problem here – movement.  Responsive movement towards the observers would certainly cause the problems you’re seeing.  But if your species is very mobile, even if movement is not responsive, it can result in data like these, hence the suggestion of using a snapshot approach in:

Buckland, S.T. 2006.  Point transect surveys for songbirds:  robust methodologies.  The Auk 123, 345-357.

This doesn’t really help you though if the movement is responsive, unless you wait at each point long enough for your study species to ignore you, then record distances to those whose position you know at a snapshot moment.  Sample size will then become an issue!

If responsive movement is a major problem, you might want to consider putting microphone arrays at each point, which can give you triangulated distances to calls/songs, and use the cue-count method for analysis.  For this, you would need to estimate the mean call rate.

Steve Buckland

